1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,120
The process of how an LM decides to call a function maybe seems a little bit mysterious initially because an LM is just trained to generate output text or output text token. So how does that work?

2
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:21,860
In this video, I'd like to walk through with you step by step what the process of getting an LM to be able to get a function called really looks like. Let's take a look.

3
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:31,400
So tools are just codes or functions that an LM can request to be executed like this get current time function that we saw from the previous video.

4
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:43,600
Now, today's leading LM's are all trained directly to use tools, but I want to walk through with you what it would look like if you had to write prompts yourself to tell it when to use tools.

5
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:51,640
And this is what we had to do in an earlier era before LM's were trained directly to use tools. And even though we don't do it exactly this way anymore.

6
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:57,580
This will hopefully give you a better understanding of the process and walk through the more modern syntax in the next video.

7
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:05,280
If you've implemented this function to get current time, then in order to give this tool to the LM, you might write a prompt like this.

8
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:12,280
You may tell it, LM, you have access to a tool called get current time. To use it, I want you to print out the following text.

9
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:19,020
Print out all caps function and then print out get current time. And if I ever see this text all caps function in the get current time,

10
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:23,100
that's when I know you want me to call the get current time function for you.

11
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:29,820
When a user asks what time is it, the LM will then realize it needs to call or request to get called the get current time function.

12
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:35,500
And so the LM will then output what it was told. It will output all caps function colon get current time.

13
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:42,420
Now, I then have to have written code to look at the output of the LM to see if there is this all caps function.

14
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:49,180
And if so, then I need to pull out the argument of this getCurrentTime to figure out what function the LLM wants to call.

15
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:57,520
And then I need to write code to actually call the getCurrentTime function and then pull out the output, which is, let's say, 8am.

16
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:07,160
And then it is the developer writing code, my code, that has to take 8am and feed that time, 8am, back onto the LLM as part of this conversational history.

17
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:12,320
And the conversational history, of course, includes the initial user prompt, the fact that it requested a function call,

18
00:02:12,420 --> 00:02:19,640
and so on. And lastly, the LLM knowing what had happened earlier, that did you ask this question, request a function call,

19
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:28,500
and then also that I call the function and return 8am. Finally, the LLM can look at all this and generate the final response, which is, it is 8am.

20
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:38,160
So to be clear, in order to call a function, the LLM doesn't call the function directly. It instead outputs something in a specific format like this

21
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:45,060
that tells me that I need to call the function for the LLM and then tell the LLM what was the output of the function it had requested.

22
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:52,360
In this example, we had given the LLM only a single function, but you can imagine if we gave it three or four functions,

23
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:57,180
we could tell it to output function in all caps, then the name of the function it once called,

24
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:03,340
and maybe even some arguments of these functions. In fact, now let's take a look at a slightly more complex example,

25
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:10,080
where the get current time function accepts an argument for the time zone at which you want the current time.

26
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:17,020
For the second example, I've written a function that gets the current time in a specified time zone,

27
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:23,740
where here the time zone is the input argument to the get current time function. So to let the LLM use this

28
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:29,020
tool to answer questions like maybe what time is it in New Zealand, because my aunt lives there and so

29
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:31,800
before I call her up, I do look up what time it is in New Zealand.

30
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:39,160
To let the LM use this tool, you might modify the system prompt to say you can use the getCurrentTimeTool for a specific time zone.

31
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:43,780
To use it, I'll put the following, getCurrentTime, and then include the time zone.

32
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:50,800
And this is an abbreviated prompt. In practice, you might put more details than this into the prompt to tell it what is the function, how to use it, and so on.

33
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:58,920
In this example, the LM will then realize it needs to fetch the time in New Zealand, and so it will generate output like this.

34
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:06,360
function, colon, getCurrentTime, Pacific slash Auckland. This is the New Zealand time zone, because Auckland is a major city in New Zealand.

35
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:16,720
Then I have the right code to search for whether or not this function all caps appeared in the LL output, and if so, then I need to pull out the function to call.

36
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:25,300
Lastly, I will then call getCurrentTime with the specified argument, which is generated by the LM, which is Pacific slash Auckland, and maybe returns as 4AM.

37
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,600
Then as usual, I feed this to the LM and the LM outputs.

38
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:34,080
It is 4AM in New Zealand. To summarize, here's the process for getting the LM to use tools.

39
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:40,220
First, you have to provide the tool to the LM, implement the function, and tell the LM that it is available.

40
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:48,840
When the LM decides to call a tool, it then generates a specific output that lets you know that you need to call the function for the LM.

41
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:58,420
Then you call the function, get this output, take the output of the function you just called, and give that output back to the LM, and the LM then uses that to go on to whatever it decides.

42
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:03,140
to do next, which in our examples in this video was to just generate the final output.

43
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:08,840
But sometimes it may even decide that the next step is to go call yet another tool and the process continues.

44
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:13,560
Now, it turns out that this all caps function syntax, it is a little bit clunky.

45
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:21,060
This is what we used to do before LM's were trained natively or to know by themselves how to request that tools be called.

46
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:27,780
With modern LM's, you don't need to tell it to output all caps function, then search the all caps function, and so on.

47
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:33,880
Instead, LM's are trained to use a specific syntax to request very clearly when it wants a tool called.

48
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:42,040
In the next video, I want to share with you what the modern syntax actually looks like for letting LM's request to tools be called.

49
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,800
Let's go on to the next video.

50
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:43,900
Let's go on to the next video.

